-- title:
--   Specht constituents of a positional product of word vectors on complementary alphabets
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $[n]=\{0,1,\dots,n-1\}$ and $S_n$ is the symmetric group on $[n]$. For $n,d\in\mathbb{N}$ let $W_{n,d}$ be the space of complex-valued functions on words $w:[n]\to[d]$, with basis vectors $e_w$ (the indicator function of $w$); $S_n$ acts by permuting positions, $(g\cdot x)(w)=x(w\circ g)$, so that $g\cdot e_w=e_{w\circ g^{-1}}$. Thus $W_{n,d}\cong(\mathbb{C}^d)^{\otimes n}$ with $S_n$ permuting the tensor factors. A Young diagram $\mu$ is a finite set of cells $(i,j)\in\mathbb{N}^2$ (row $i$, column $j$, both counted from $0$) that is closed under moving up or to the left; $\mu_i$ is the length of row $i$, $|\mu|$ the number of cells and $\ell(\mu)$ the number of nonempty rows. A tableau of shape $\mu$ on $n$ positions is a bijection $t:[n]\to\mu$ (so $n=|\mu|$). Its row word $r_t:[n]\to[\ell(\mu)]$ sends $i$ to the row of the cell $t(i)$, and its column group $C_t\le S_n$ consists of the permutations $g$ such that $t(g(i))$ lies in the same column as $t(i)$ for every $i$. The polytabloid of $t$ is $e_t=\sum_{g\in C_t}\operatorname{sgn}(g)\,g\cdot e_{r_t}\in W_{n,\ell(\mu)}$, and the Specht module $S^t=\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{C}}\{g\cdot e_t:g\in S_n\}\subseteq W_{n,\ell(\mu)}$ is a representation of $S_n$ under the restricted action.
--
--   For $z\in W_{n,d}$ let $\mathbb{C}[S_n]z=\operatorname{span}\{g\cdot z:g\in S_n\}$ be the cyclic subrepresentation generated by $z$, and for representations $X,Y$ of a group $G$ let $\operatorname{Hom}_G(X,Y)$ be the space of $G$-equivariant linear maps. A pair of Young diagrams $\eta\subseteq\mu$ is a horizontal strip if no two cells of $\mu\setminus\eta$ lie in the same column. For a list $\mathbf b=(b_0,\dots,b_{k-1})$ of natural numbers, a sized strip chain from $\nu$ to $\mu$ with sizes $\mathbf b$ is a chain of Young diagrams $\nu=\eta_0\subseteq\eta_1\subseteq\cdots\subseteq\eta_k=\mu$ such that every step $\eta_j\subseteq\eta_{j+1}$ is a horizontal strip with $|\eta_{j+1}|=|\eta_j|+b_j$.
--
--   Let $\varepsilon:[n]\to[a]\sqcup[b]$ be a bijection. For a word $w:[n]\to[d]$ let $w_L(i)=w(\varepsilon^{-1}(\mathrm{inl}\,i))$ and $w_R(j)=w(\varepsilon^{-1}(\mathrm{inr}\,j))$ be its subwords on the left and right blocks, and for $x\in W_{a,d}$, $y\in W_{b,d}$ define the positional product $x\boxtimes_\varepsilon y\in W_{n,d}$ by $(x\boxtimes_\varepsilon y)(w)=x(w_L)\,y(w_R)$. Fix a set of letters $A\subseteq[d]$.
--
--   Assume that $\nu$ and $\mu$ are joined by a sized strip chain with sizes $\mathbf b$ of length $k$; that $s$ is a tableau of shape $\nu$ on $a$ positions and $t$ a tableau of shape $\mu$ on $n$ positions; that $v\in W_{a,d}$ vanishes on every word containing a letter outside $A$, and $u\in W_{b,d}$ vanishes on every word containing a letter in $A$; that $\operatorname{Hom}_{S_a}(S^s,\mathbb{C}[S_a]v)\neq0$; and that $\operatorname{Hom}_{S_b}(S^r,\mathbb{C}[S_b]u)\neq0$ for every Young diagram $\eta$ with $\ell(\eta)\le k$ and every tableau $r$ of shape $\eta$ on $b$ positions. Then
--
--   $$
--   \operatorname{Hom}_{S_n}\bigl(S^t,\ \mathbb{C}[S_n]\,(v\boxtimes_\varepsilon u)\bigr)\neq0.
--   $$
--
--   In words: if $v$, written in the letters of $A$, generates a copy of $S^\nu$, and $u$, written in the complementary letters, generates every Specht module with at most $k$ rows, then the product $v\boxtimes_\varepsilon u$ generates $S^\mu$ for every $\mu$ obtained from $\nu$ by adding $k$ successive horizontal strips of the given sizes. This is the step that propagates Specht constituents through a band of strips when a cyclic module is assembled from pieces on disjoint alphabets.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Nonvanishing of a Hom space is expressed by a nonzero `Representation.IntertwiningMap` (for the hypothesis on $v$, a given nonzero map `f`); $\mathbb{C}[S_n]z$ is `cyclic (wordRep n d) z`, the alphabet conditions are membership in `alphabetSub`, $v\boxtimes_\varepsilon u$ is `positionProduct e v u`, and $\ell(\eta)\le k$ is `η.colLen 0 ≤ bs.length`. The letter set $A$ is a predicate on `Fin d`.
